# deploy/herald-translate-check.service
#
# systemd --user unit for the weekly GLOSSARY digest — two commands, one fire, one reading slot.
#
#   1. `pnpm translate:check --notify` asks the two questions src/cli/translate-check.ts's own doc
#      comment names: did our draft use the decided term the source called for (`checkGlossary`), and
#      which decided terms do the humans keep dropping once a post actually goes out
#      (`checkPublishedOverrides`).
#   2. `pnpm glossary:mine --notify` asks the question neither of those can: which terms have never
#      been decided at all. Un-glossed proper nouns the source keeps repeating, word-level
#      substitutions a human made between our draft and the published post, both cross-validated
#      against the @0xMantleKR reference corpus (src/domain/translation/glossaryMining.ts).
#
# Both are read-only against the pipeline; the only thing written anywhere is `glossary:mine`'s review
# file under HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR (see that variable below, which this unit did NOT set until the second
# command joined it).
#
# WHY THEY SHARE A UNIT rather than getting a fifth timer. They are two halves of one question — "is
# the glossary right, and is it complete?" — read by the same person in the same sitting, and they are
# driven by the same two slow inputs (a human editing the glossary, and `x:reconcile` capturing a
# published text). A separate timer would buy an independent schedule nobody wants and cost a second
# weekly interruption; `Type=oneshot` runs multiple ExecStart= lines in order, which is exactly the
# "one digest slot, two related reports" shape this wants. The ordering consequence is deliberate too:
# a oneshot stops at the first ExecStart= that fails, so a broken `translate:check` (no database, no
# glossary) skips the mine — correct, because both read the same two things and the mine would fail
# for the same reason one line later, with a second, more confusing alert.
#
# Why it needs a schedule at all: the drift `translate:check` catches is invisible one item at a time
# — `narrative` rendered 이야기 is a perfectly good sentence, wrong only against a decision recorded
# elsewhere. Measured on 2026-08-07, a batch of nineteen translations had drifted from four decided
# terms and every individual sentence read fine. And the gap `glossary:mine` catches is invisible from
# every direction: on 2026-08-11 a single hand-run of the same three signals took the glossary from 96
# entries to 106, not because anyone lacked a place to type them but because nobody knew which ten
# terms to type. Both commands then get run when somebody remembers, which for reports whose entire
# value is catching slow drift is close to not at all.
#
# WHAT THIS UNIT NEVER DOES: change anything a human curates, and never gate. Neither command writes a
# database row or changes a status, and neither exits non-zero on a finding — a glossary has real
# exceptions and an un-decided term is a question, so these are lists a human reads before 1차 검수,
# not gates. That has a useful consequence for the hook below: a non-zero exit from this unit can only
# ever mean a run itself broke (no database, no glossary, timeout), never that a report had something
# in it. The alert is therefore never a weekly false alarm, and the findings reach a human the other
# way — each command's `--notify` sends at most one grouped ops message of its own
# (`overrideNotification`, src/cli/translateCheckReport.ts; `miningNotification`,
# src/cli/glossaryMineReport.ts), while `translate:check`'s drift half deliberately never pages and is
# read out of the run log this unit's wrapper keeps.
#
# NOT installed by any committed script. Copying this into ~/.config/systemd/user/ and running
# `systemctl --user daemon-reload` is a human-supervised step (see the team runbook), same as the
# four units already in this directory.
#
# Machine-specific by design, not templated: node and pnpm are reached through `%h/.herald/bin` on
# the one machine this runs on — see the PATH= comment below, and herald-watch.service's longer
# version of it.

[Unit]
Description=Mantle KR Herald weekly glossary digest — `pnpm translate:check --notify` then `pnpm glossary:mine --notify`
# The same templated hook the other four scheduled units use. `%n` expands to this unit's own name
# before systemd resolves the target, so the alert names this unit and tails this unit's journal, not
# another's — the `OnFailure=bar@%n.service` idiom `man systemd.unit` documents, and the reason the
# hook became a template at all (see deploy/herald-notify-failure@.service's header).
#
# Worth more here than the once-a-day units it copies, not less, and for a reason specific to what
# `--notify` sends: `overrideNotification` returns undefined when there are no overrides and
# `miningNotification` returns undefined when there are no candidates, so a healthy week with a clean,
# complete glossary sends NOTHING from either command. Silence is therefore the normal successful
# outcome, which means silence cannot also be how a dead fire announces itself. This hook is the only
# thing that separates the two, for seven days at a time.
OnFailure=herald-notify-failure@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The deploy checkout — merged `main` only. Same reasoning as every other unit: whatever is checked
# out here IS what runs at the next fire, and on 2026-08-07 a unit pointed at the development
# checkout ran an unmerged branch's query against production. tests/deploy/workingDirectory.test.ts
# fails if this ever names a development checkout.
#
# It decides more than the code for THIS command. `translation/glossary.json` is resolved relative to
# the repo root (`paths.translationConfigDir`, src/paths.ts), so this directory is also where the
# glossary the report is measured against comes from — the copy `pnpm deploy:freeze` lands here from
# the development tree's git-ignored `translation/`, not whatever anyone edited since. A glossary
# edit therefore reaches this report at the next deploy, not the next fire, which is the same
# deliberate delay every other steering value has had since 2026-08-09.
WorkingDirectory=%h/.herald/app

# node and pnpm are reached through %h/.herald/bin, a directory of symlinks, NOT an nvm version
# directory — see herald-watch.service's own PATH comment for the 2026-08-09 rebuild that made this
# necessary. node must be reachable even though pnpm is named by absolute path below: pnpm's own file
# starts `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and `env` resolves that through this PATH. `claude` is deliberately
# not on this path: neither command spawns an agent, exactly like herald-creds.service and
# herald-x-reconcile.service and unlike the two units that do. `glossary:mine` is worth checking
# against that claim rather than assuming it — everything it does is regexes over strings, and the
# judgement calls it cannot make are the ones it leaves blank for a human in the review file.
Environment=PATH=%h/.herald/bin:/home/kyle/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# This line is a REVERSAL, and the reasoning it replaces is worth keeping visible. Until
# `glossary:mine` joined this unit there was no HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR here, argued at length and correctly:
# `translate:check` reads exactly two things, and neither is OUTPUT_DIR-relative. Its translations come
# from `createStores(db).translationStore` — `PgTranslationStore`, Postgres, not OUTPUT_DIR's
# `translations/translations.json`, which stopped being the ledger when the eleven stores moved (see
# src/cli/stores.ts). Its glossary comes from `new JsonGlossaryStore(paths.translationConfigDir)`, and
# that path is `join(REPO_ROOT, "translation")` — repo-root-relative, so the variable would not have
# redirected it even if someone set it hoping to. Setting it then would have implied an artifact tree
# that run had no relationship with.
#
# The second ExecStart= changes that fact, so the line changes with it. `glossary:mine` reads two more
# things and WRITES one, and all three are OUTPUT_DIR-relative:
#
#   reads  `$OUTPUT_DIR/x/reference/items.json` + `runs.json` — the @0xMantleKR corpus its
#          cross-validation grades against, and the run ledger it reads staleness out of. Only
#          `collect:reference` writes those, and only by hand. Without this variable the mine would
#          read `%h/.herald/app/output/x/reference/` — which does not exist — and degrade to "no
#          corpus" every single week, silently grading every candidate B forever.
#
#          This variable is necessary and was not sufficient, which cost the first real fire. A
#          hand-run `pnpm collect:reference` sets no HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR, so it writes into the
#          DEVELOPMENT checkout's `<repo>/output/x/reference/` — not into this root. On 2026-08-11
#          this directory had never been written to at all, cross-validation was blind, and the run
#          graded every candidate B with nothing but its own `참조 코퍼스 없음` line to say so.
#          `bash deploy/herald-deploy.sh` now copies the corpus across as its step 4
#          (deploy/herald-copy-corpus.sh) — the same deploy-time copy the steering config gets, for
#          the same reason: production must not read live files out of the tree someone edits.
#   writes `$OUTPUT_DIR/glossary/candidates-<YYYY-MM-DD>.json`, the review file that IS this job's
#          deliverable. Without the variable it would land inside the deploy checkout, which
#          `deploy/herald-deploy.sh` owns and `pnpm deploy:check` refuses to deploy over when dirty.
#
# %h/.herald/output, the same root herald-watch.service and herald-convert.service already name, so
# every scheduled command shares one artifact tree and the deploy has one place to put the corpus in.
# It is emphatically NOT a corpus this scheduler collected — nothing here runs `collect:reference`,
# by design (see HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES below). The review file's absolute
# path is printed on stdout and carried in the Telegram notice for exactly this reason: the path an
# interactive `pnpm glossary:mine` writes is NOT this one, and an alert that says "open the draft"
# without saying which one is a trap.
Environment=HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR=%h/.herald/output

# No HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE, HERALD_WATCH_BATCH, HERALD_CONVERT_BATCH or HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES.
# All four bound a `--limit` or a page count belonging to translate:prepare/translate:align,
# convert:prepare or collect, and neither command here runs any of them — `translate:check` selects
# with its own `--status`/`--since` flags, which this unit deliberately does not pass (see ExecStart=
# below), and `glossary:mine` has no selection flags at all. HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES is the one worth
# naming twice: `glossary:mine` deliberately does NOT collect. Refreshing the reference corpus weekly
# would spend twitterapi.io budget on data that is overwhelmingly historical, so the job reads whatever
# `collect:reference` last left and says out loud when that is stale (28 days,
# REFERENCE_STALE_AFTER_DAYS) instead of quietly fixing it.

# Exactly two lines — DATABASE_URL and HERALD_DB_ENV=production — chmod 600, created by Kyle by hand
# (a production connection string does not pass through an agent session). This unit needs it for the
# reason a read-only command needs it most: without it the fire succeeds, exits 0, and reports
# cheerfully on the local Docker database's handful of rows instead of production's — a green weekly
# digest about the wrong data, and the only sign is the database name on each command's first line,
# which nobody reads on a run that found nothing. Everything else comes from the repo's own .env via
# each command's `tsx --env-file-if-exists=.env` (both scripts carry it in package.json), including the
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_OPS that `--notify` needs; a shell-exported variable wins
# over Node's --env-file (verified on this machine), so these two override .env's local-Docker
# DATABASE_URL without this unit ever touching .env itself.
EnvironmentFile=%h/.herald/prod.env

# Outer bound on a wedged run. For Type=oneshot this covers the WHOLE ExecStart= sequence, not each
# line, so adding the second command re-opened this number — and unusually for this directory it is
# still the ALERT path that sizes it, now twice over. Four parts:
#
#   120s — translate:check's database half. One `translationStore.loadAll()` plus connect and close;
#          the checks themselves are in-memory string work over rows already fetched, and the glossary
#          is a local file read. What this has to absorb is a cold Neon compute: at this timer's Monday
#          06:53 slot the last thing to touch the database was the 06:41 reconcile, and Neon
#          autosuspends after about five minutes, so essentially every fire pays a wake-up.
#          herald-convert.service budgets 240s for three round trips including that wake; this is one
#          query, so 120s is the same generosity at a third of the traffic.
#
#   300s — translate:check's Telegram half. `notifyOps` (src/shared/notifyOps.ts) calls global `fetch`
#          with no timeout of its own, so a Telegram endpoint that accepts the connection and then
#          never answers is bounded only by undici's 300s headers timeout. Reaching that timeout is the
#          GOOD outcome: notifyOps never throws — it logs `[notifyOps] failed to notify` and returns —
#          so the run still exits 0 and the report is still in the run log. A bound below 300 would
#          instead SIGTERM the process mid-send, which turns a working check into a failed unit and
#          fires the OnFailure= hook to say the glossary check failed, when the check had already
#          finished and printed — a false alarm about the one subsystem (Telegram) that is demonstrably
#          down and therefore least likely to deliver the alarm anyway.
#
#   120s — glossary:mine's database half. TWO queries, not one — `collectionRepository.loadAll()`
#          (every thread in `x_threads`, ~523 tweets of JSON, by far the larger payload) and a second
#          `translationStore.loadAll()` — but no cold-start to pay: translate:check woke the compute
#          one command earlier and Neon's autosuspend is minutes away. Two warm queries against one
#          cold query is the same 120s of headroom, spent differently. The mining itself is regexes
#          over strings already in memory; the corpus and the review file are local disk.
#
#   300s — glossary:mine's Telegram half, identical reasoning to translate:check's, and NOT shared with
#          it. Two separate processes each make their own send, so a Telegram outage can hang both,
#          one after the other.
#
# 120 + 300 + 120 + 300 = 840. The retry inside notifyOps does not double either 300: a second attempt
# only happens when the first got a real response back, and a response means it did not hang. The one
# shape that overruns this is Telegram answering slowly AND then hanging the plain-text retry, and at
# that point being killed is the right answer.
#
# The upper bound the rest of this directory argues for — timeout at or under half the fire period, or
# one wedged run holds the unit active across the next OnCalendar= and systemd skips it — is still
# nowhere near binding: 840s against a 604800s week is 1/720th of the period.
TimeoutStartSec=840

# Wrapped in herald-run-logged.sh for the reason every other unit is, and more so than any of them:
# journald on this box holds roughly eight minutes of history (it rotates on every backwards clock
# step, and this machine's WSL2 host sync and timesyncd both step the clock constantly), so a report
# printed at 06:53 on Monday is unreadable by 07:05 and the next one is seven days away. This is also
# the only unit here whose SUCCESSFUL output is the entire point — the Telegram notices are summaries,
# and `%h/.herald/logs/herald-translate-check/` is where the full grouped list of drifts and overrides
# actually lives when someone wants to read them item by item, alongside the line naming the review
# file `glossary:mine` wrote.
#
# Argument order is that wrapper's contract, not a convention: `$1` is the unit name and everything
# after it is the command, run verbatim. Reversed, the wrapper names its log directory after pnpm and
# tries to run the unit name as a command (measured: exit 127). `%n` also keys the per-unit log
# directory, the same key deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh derives its fallback excerpt from, so the
# two agree without either hardcoding a unit list. tests/deploy/runLogging.test.ts holds the property
# that matters most — the command's exit status reaches systemd unchanged, since a wrapper that
# swallowed it would switch this unit's alert off permanently and invisibly.
#
# `--notify` is not optional decoration, and it is spelled out here for the reason
# herald-x-reconcile.service spells out `--yes`: it is off by default (an interactive run stays
# stdout-only), so without it every fire writes a report into a log nobody is tailing and exits 0 — a
# scheduler that looks armed and delivers nothing. It sends one grouped ops message for the
# published-override half and nothing at all when there are none, so the flag costs nothing on a
# clean week and is the only reason a dirty one reaches anybody.
#
# No `--status`, no `--since`, no `--published`. `--status`/`--since` narrow the row set, and the
# standing whole-ledger view is exactly what a weekly digest is for — a narrowed one would answer
# "did last week drift?" while the question this report exists for is "what has drifted?".
# `--published` is not passed either: it would restrict question 1 to rows that already carry a
# published text and skip the rest, and the published-override half of the report (question 2) runs
# unconditionally anyway, so it would cost coverage and buy nothing. Anyone who wants either view
# runs the command by hand — this unit's job is the standing one.
#
# Ordering note for whoever installs this: the wrapper lives in the deploy checkout, so
# `bash deploy/herald-deploy.sh` must have run (landing this commit in %h/.herald/app) BEFORE this
# unit file is copied into ~/.config/systemd/user/.
ExecStart=%h/.herald/app/deploy/herald-run-logged.sh %n %h/.herald/bin/pnpm translate:check --notify

# The second half of the digest, through the SAME wrapper with the same %n. Two consequences worth
# stating, because both are properties of herald-run-logged.sh rather than of this file:
#
#   - TWO log files per fire, not one. The wrapper stamps `RUN_STAMP` per process, so each ExecStart=
#     gets its own `%h/.herald/logs/herald-translate-check/<stamp>.log` with its own started/exited
#     header pair. They sort adjacent (same fire, seconds apart) and carry the same `invocation` id —
#     systemd assigns one per unit START, not per Exec line — so the pair is still attributable to one
#     fire, which is exactly what deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh's attribution check needs.
#   - The 60-run retention cap is per unit directory, so this unit's history is now ~30 fires rather
#     than 60. At a weekly cadence that is still over half a year, which is far past the point anyone
#     reads back to.
#
# On a failing first command the second never runs, so the newest file by name IS the failure — the
# ordering the hook assumes still holds.
#
# `--notify` for the same reason the line above carries it: off by default, so without it every fire
# writes a review file into a tree nobody is watching and exits 0. It sends one grouped message naming
# the tiers and the review file's absolute path, and NOTHING when there are no candidates — so a clean
# week still costs zero interruptions even though this unit now runs two notifying commands.
#
# No flags beyond it, and there are none to pass: `glossary:mine` has no `--since`, no `--status` and
# no `--limit`. The standing whole-ledger view is the only view it has, deliberately, for the same
# reason `translate:check` is not narrowed here — the question is "what has never been decided?", not
# "what came up last week?".
ExecStart=%h/.herald/app/deploy/herald-run-logged.sh %n %h/.herald/bin/pnpm glossary:mine --notify
